\subsection{Revisiting the Graduality Proof}

Having described the double category in which the casts live, we now
return to the graduality proof.  We need to verify that the morphisms
of predomains/error domains used to define the cast morphisms do in
fact extend to perturbations.

We also need to check that the type constructors act on squares.

\subsubsection{Representability}

Recall that the notion of \emph{quasi}-representability of a relation involves a
perturbation corresponding to the morphism opposite the cast. For each
of the \upl, \upr, \dnl, and \dnr rules there is a distinguished
perturbation that appears in the relevant square.
%
In the double category of casts we have just defined, the
perturbations are moved into the definition of the squares themselves.
Thus, we can define representability of a relation as the familiar
notion of representability of a horizontal morphism in a double
category. In particular:

\begin{definition}
  Let $A$ and $A'$ be value objects, and let $c : A \rel A'$ be a
  predomain relation.  We say that $c$ is \emph{left-representable by}
  a perturbation-preserving morphism $e_c : A \to A'$ if there are
  squares $\upl : e_c \ltsqdelay{c}{r(A')} \id_{A'}$ and $\upr : \id_A
  \ltsqdelay{r(A)}{c} e_c$. We say that $c$ is \emph{left-representable}
  if it is left-representable by $e_c$ for some $e_c$.

  We say that $c$ is \emph{right-representable by} a
  perturbation-preserving morphism $p_c : A' \to A$ if there are
  squares $\dnr : \id_A \ltsqdelay{c}{r(A)} p_c$ and
  $\dnl : p_c \ltsqdelay{r(A')}{c} \id_{A'}$.

  We define the notion of representability for error domain relations
  analogously.
  
\end{definition}

An important consequence of this definition is that if $c$ is left
(resp. right) representable, then it is actually determined up to
perturbation by the embedding (resp. projection) morphism.  More
specifically, suppose $c$ is left-representable by a morphism $e_c$.
Consider the relation $\restr{\ltdyn_{A'}}{e_c}$, i.e., the
restriction of the identity relation on $A'$ along the morphism $e_c$.
The fact that $c$ is left-representable by $e_c$ implies that $c$ is
\emph{order-equivalent} to $\restr{\ltdyn_{A'}}{e_c}$, as shown in the
lemma below.

\begin{lemma}
  Let $c : A \rel A'$ be a predomain relation. If $c$ is left-representable by $e_c$,
  then there is a perturbed square $\id \ltsqdelay{c}{\restr{\ltdyn_{A'}}{e_c}} \id$
  and a perturbed square $\id \ltsqdelay{\restr{\ltdyn_{A'}}{e_c}}{c} \id$.
\end{lemma}
